    <Member MemberName="RequestSync">
      <MemberSignature Language="C#" Value="public void RequestSync ();" />
      <MemberSignature Language="ILAsm" Value=".method public hidebysig newslot virtual instance void RequestSync() cil managed" />
      <MemberSignature Language="DocId" Value="M:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationHost.RequestSync" />
      <MemberSignature Language="VB.NET" Value="Public Sub RequestSync ()" />
      <MemberSignature Language="C++ CLI" Value="public:&#xA; void RequestSync();" />
      <MemberSignature Language="F#" Value="abstract member RequestSync : unit -&gt; unit" Usage="iModalNavigationHost.RequestSync " />
      <MemberType>Method</MemberType>
      <AssemblyInfo>
        <AssemblyName>Microsoft.Maui.Controls</AssemblyName>
        <AssemblyVersion>11.0.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
      </AssemblyInfo>
      <ReturnValue>
        <ReturnType>System.Void</ReturnType>
      </ReturnValue>
      <Parameters />
      <Docs>
        <summary>
            Asks the framework to re-run the reconciliation loop that compares the requested modal stack
            with <see cref="P:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationHost.PlatformModalStack" /> and issues any push or pop that is still outstanding.
            </summary>
        <remarks>
          <para>
            Call this when <see cref="P:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationPlatform.IsReady" /> transitions from
            <see langword="false" /> to <see langword="true" />. The framework does not poll
            <see cref="P:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationPlatform.IsReady" />, so a platform that defers readiness must
            notify the framework through this method or queued modals will never be presented.
            </para>
          <para>
            This is safe to call from any thread. When the caller is not already on the window's UI
            thread and the window has a dispatcher, the work is marshalled there and this method returns
            immediately. When called <b>on</b> the UI thread the reconciliation starts synchronously,
            which means
            <see cref="P:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationPlatform.IsReady" /> and possibly
            <see cref="M:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationPlatform.PushModalAsync(Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Page,System.Boolean)" /> or
            <see cref="M:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Platform.IModalNavigationPlatform.PopModalAsync(Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Page,System.Boolean)" /> can be re-entered before
            this method returns. Do not call it while holding a lock, and finish mutating your own state
            before calling it.
            </para>
          <para>
            A window created on a thread without a dispatcher has no UI-thread target to marshal to. In
            that unusual case the framework runs the request inline as a best effort, so a backend that
            supports such windows should call this method from its UI thread.
            </para>
          <para>
            A marshalled request is bound to the window's current handler scope. If the window is
            destroyed, or its handler is replaced, before the request runs, the request is dropped rather
            than executed against a torn-down window or a service scope that no longer exists. Calling
            this after disposal is therefore harmless but has no effect.
            </para>
        </remarks>
      </Docs>
    </Member>
